# Test: `directive scheduler=<mode>` REJECTS an unknown mode (E_UnknownDirective).
#
# The valid set is {pool, thread, inline, single}, CASE-SENSITIVE. Rust gg
# validates it in `src/semantic/mod.rs`; the self-host validates it in
# `validate_directives` (`self_host_typechecker/resolve.gg`, twinned in
# `self_host_resolver/resolve.gg`), and the two name lists are compared
# mechanically by `directive_name_sets_agree_across_lanes` in tests/lints.rs.
# That rejection is what makes each lowerer's unhandled-mode arm unreachable —
# `src/ir/lowering/mod.rs`'s `_ => {}` and `self_host_lowerer/lower.gg`'s missing
# `else`. Without it a typo'd mode compiles and runs under the default pool
# scheduler, which for an async program silently changes the interleaving.
#
# ⊕ THIS IS NOW A TWO-LANE PIN. It was a one-lane pin whose green told you
# nothing about the other lane: the self-host performed NO directive validation
# at all and compiled, linked and RAN this very file, rc 0 at every stage. The
# self-host half is `sh_rejects_unknown_scheduler_mode` (over
# `sh_reject_unknown_scheduler_mode.gg`, an async program where the wrong
# scheduler is observable), and the same cell is adjudicated on all three
# production lanes — C, LLVM and self-host — by
# `spectests/run/reject_directive_scheduler_unknown_value.gg`.
#
# The sibling `spectests/run/reject_directive_unknown_name.gg` pins an unknown
# directive NAME; this one pins an unknown directive VALUE, a separate arm of the
# same validation.

directive scheduler=bogus

async void main():
    print("REACHED — the scheduler directive was NOT validated on this lane")
